<!-- ============================================================
     CVE-2026-53359 "Januscape" - KVM /dev/kvm Access and Module Load Detection
     Supplementary auditd-based rules (companion to rules 104135-104141)

     Parent file: staged/januscape_kvm_cve_2026_53359_vm_escape.xml
     Parent issue: ESKA-614 | This issue: ESKA-618

     MITRE ATT&CK:
       T1611  - Escape to Host (TA0004)
       T1068  - Exploitation for Privilege Escalation (TA0004)
       T1082  - System Information Discovery (TA0007)

     Rule IDs: 104142-104144
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-22
     References:
       - https://thehackernews.com/2026/07/16-year-old-linux-kvm-flaw-lets-guest.html
       - https://tuxcare.com/blog/januscape-exposes-the-kvm-shadow-paging-bug-that-kept-coming-back/
       - https://forum.proxmox.com/threads/are-there-mitigations-available-for-cve-2026-53359-januscape.184874/

     Detection rationale:
       Rules 104135-104141 (ESKA-614) cover KVM shadow MMU kernel log signals
       (GFN mismatch, role confusion, kernel BUG, QEMU crash, kernel panic).

       These supplementary rules cover two additional detection surfaces:

       Surface A - unexpected /dev/kvm access
         On a hardened KVM host, /dev/kvm should only be opened by:
           - /usr/bin/qemu-system-x86_64 (QEMU process, usually as root or libvirt-qemu)
           - /usr/lib/qemu/qemu-kvm
           - libvirt management layer
         Any other binary opening /dev/kvm is anomalous and could indicate:
           a) A VM-escaped process probing the host's KVM control surface
           b) An attacker tool enumerating KVM capabilities before privilege escalation
           c) A debugging tool (kvmtool, kvm-unit-tests) - should be in change window

       Surface B - KVM module loaded outside of boot context
         kvm_intel/kvm_amd module loading mid-session (outside boot) may indicate:
           a) A post-exploit actor reloading KVM with modified parameters (e.g., nested=1)
           b) An operator action to enable nested virtualisation (change-managed, low risk)
         The audit rule (init_module/finit_module with kvm_dev_access key) fires for
         both insmod and modprobe paths.

     Log source prerequisites - add to all Proxmox/KVM hosts via Ansible (inframan):

       File: /etc/audit/rules.d/90-januscape-kvm.rules

         # Detect /dev/kvm opened by any non-root process (kvm_dev_access_nonroot key)
         # Covers open, openat, openat2 syscalls targeting /dev/kvm
         -a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S open,openat,openat2 -F path=/dev/kvm -F uid!=0 -k kvm_dev_access_nonroot

         # Detect kvm_intel/kvm_amd module load outside of boot (kvm_module_load key)
         -a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S init_module,finit_module -k kvm_module_load

       These rules emit SYSCALL+PATH audit records. Wazuh's auditd decoder extracts
       audit.key and populates audit.exe from the SYSCALL exe field.

     Severity rationale:
        0  - Base anchor rules (internal routing only, no alert)
        9  - /dev/kvm opened by non-root process (low confidence; libvirt-qemu is normal)
       13  - /dev/kvm opened by non-QEMU, non-libvirt process (high confidence anomaly)
       10  - KVM module loaded outside boot context (configuration change, warrants review)
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="privilege_escalation,vm_escape,kvm,januscape,cve_2026_53359,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer A1: Base anchor - /dev/kvm access by non-root process
       Rule ID: 104142

       Auditd fires kvm_dev_access_nonroot on any open/openat/openat2
       syscall against /dev/kvm by a non-root UID. This rule is a silent
       anchor (level 0) that routes all such events into the chain for
       the more specific rules below.

       Audit record example:
         type=SYSCALL msg=audit(1753142400.123:456): arch=c000003e syscall=257
           success=yes exit=3 a0=ffffff9c a1=55... a2=80000 a3=0
           items=1 ppid=1234 pid=5678 auid=1000 uid=1000 gid=1000
           euid=1000 ... comm="exploit-kvm" exe="/tmp/exploit-kvm"
           key="kvm_dev_access_nonroot"
         type=PATH msg=audit(1753142400.123:456): item=0 name="/dev/kvm"
           inode=... dev=... mode=020660 ...
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104142" level="0">
    <decoded_as>auditd</decoded_as>
    <field name="audit.key">kvm_dev_access_nonroot</field>
    <description>Januscape CVE-2026-53359: /dev/kvm opened by non-root process (base anchor) (T1611/T1068)</description>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer A2: /dev/kvm opened by a non-QEMU, non-libvirt process
       Rule ID: 104143

       Fires when a non-root process opens /dev/kvm AND the executable
       is not a known-legitimate KVM frontend (QEMU or libvirt). The
       pcre2 negative match excludes the standard QEMU binary paths and
       libvirt daemon paths commonly found on Proxmox/Debian systems.

       False-positive sources:
         - KVM unit-test frameworks (kvm-unit-tests) during maintenance
         - kvmtool (lightweight KVM launcher)
         - Custom VM launchers
         - Action: verify against authorized change management

       Example anomalous access:
         exe="/tmp/escaped-process" or exe="/home/user/kvm-tool"
         uid=1001 (non-root, non-libvirt-qemu)
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104143" level="13">
    <if_sid>104142</if_sid>
    <field name="audit.exe" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:qemu-system-x86_64|qemu-system-aarch64|qemu-kvm|qemu-system-arm|libvirt|virt-manager|virsh|vhost-net)</field>
    <description>Januscape CVE-2026-53359: /dev/kvm opened by non-QEMU process exe=$(audit.exe) uid=$(audit.uid) - unexpected KVM device access; may indicate VM-escape attempt or unauthorized hypervisor interaction (T1611/T1068)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1068</id>
      <id>T1611</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>privilege_escalation,vm_escape,kvm,januscape,cve_2026_53359,kvm_dev_access,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer B: KVM kernel module loaded outside of boot context
       Rule ID: 104144

       Matches auditd init_module / finit_module syscalls tagged with
       the kvm_module_load key. An operator loading kvm_intel/kvm_amd
       mid-session (e.g., to enable nested=1) is expected to be rare
       and change-managed; an attacker may reload the module with
       modified parameters to re-enable the Januscape attack surface
       after an operator disabled nested virtualisation as a workaround.

       This rule fires at level 10 (warrants investigation, not critical)
       because planned operator actions are the most likely trigger.

       Audit record example:
         type=SYSCALL ... syscall=313 (finit_module) ... uid=0 ...
           key="kvm_module_load"
         type=PATH ... name="/lib/modules/.../kvm_intel.ko" ...

       Correlate with rule 104141 (nested KVM enabled) to confirm
       exploitation-enabling parameter was set.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104144" level="10">
    <decoded_as>auditd</decoded_as>
    <field name="audit.key">kvm_module_load</field>
    <description>Januscape CVE-2026-53359: KVM kernel module (kvm_intel/kvm_amd) loaded outside of boot context by uid=$(audit.uid) - correlate with rule 104141 (nested KVM enabled) to determine if exploitation prerequisites were set (T1611)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1068</id>
      <id>T1611</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>privilege_escalation,vm_escape,kvm,januscape,cve_2026_53359,kvm_module_load,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
